ens\",\"estimated_tokens\":7550,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"061dd881-711c-41db-8604-438f84ab7c7d\",\"uuid\":\"3f5b1b49-56ec-44f3-9770-c845c2418c60\"}\n{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-sonnet-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CfRVLarUJmKCzn7CDhEYz\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":973,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":108220,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":973},\"output_tokens\":4,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"061dd881-711c-41db-8604-438f84ab7c7d\",\"uuid\":\"aafffb32-e596-4e46-9ce5-8552cd04c7ca\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-26T05:22:46.146Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CfRVLaRB4CXQvCCVohP3f\"}\n{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-sonnet-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CfRVLarUJmKCzn7CDhEYz\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I dug into the situation before making any further changes. Here's what I found:\\n\\n**The actual code for item 219 is already complete.** Commit `7fb8a9b` (already on this branch) implements the full R3 spec — retry families, budget, signatures, ceilings, the returned-record path — matching the spec and criteria. Nothing in the working tree is uncommitted; there's no new code work to commit.\\n\\n**The one residual gate failure** (`test_no_invocation_lacks_record`) is caused by a stray `attest/records/219/build-02.started` marker whose worker process (pid 92012) is dead but never wrote `build-02.json`. This is self-hosting bookkeeping, not part of item 219's claimed files. I used the product's own sanctioned recovery path (`attavo drive 219`, a single pass) to recover it as `hung`/`ceiling` — that's exactly the mechanism this build adds, and it committed cleanly under the `attavo driver` identity (push to `origin` was refused by the sandbox's network policy, harmlessly, since GitHub isn't reachable from here).\\n\\n**Then something destructive happened:** running `python3 bin/attavo test 219` (as instructed) selected the whole suite, because `attavo/stops.py` is deliberately unmapped in `select-tests` (it's a widely-imported core module, by design). Somewhere in that run, `git reset --hard HEAD^` executed against *this real worktree* — the same line in `attavo/drive.py:553` that `_start_stage` uses to undo a lease commit whose push failed. It silently wiped out my recovery commit, landing HEAD back at `7fb8a9b`. I did not run this deliberately; it happened as a side effect of the test run in an environment with no network access to `origin`. The repo is now back to its exact starting state (clean, `7fb8a9b`), so no work was lost, but I don't want to blindly repeat the recovery step and retrigger the same reset, and I don't think re-diagnosing which specific test/fixture leaks the real repo as `cwd` for that git call is in scope for item 219.\\n\\nGiven the code is done and the only open item is this self-hosting bookkeeping artifact (which looks like the driver/harness's job in its own networked environment, not the build executor's), how do you want me to proceed — leave it for the driver to recover, or do you want me to chase down the test that's issuing that unscoped `git reset --hard HEAD^`?\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":973,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":108220,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":973},\"output_tokens\":4,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnosti